      "headline": "IRAN STRIKES QATARI TANKER NEAR STRAIT OF HORMUZ, U.S. REVOKES IRAN'S OIL SALE LICENSE",
      "body": "Iran's Revolutionary Guard hit the Qatari LNG carrier Al-Rekayyat and a Saudi-flagged crude tanker near the Strait of Hormuz Tuesday, setting one vessel ablaze. Brent crude settled up 3% to $74.16 and WTI rose 2.8% to $70.44, then extended gains after hours — Brent to $76.04, WTI to $72.25 — once Washington revoked Iran's authorization to sell its oil, threatening the month-old truce that ended the four-month U.S.-Israel-Iran war.",
      "explain": "The Strait carries roughly 20% of global oil flow through a chokepoint just 21 miles wide at its narrowest, so any attack there prices in a war-risk premium immediately, well before a single barrel of actual supply is lost. Revoking Iran's sale license adds a second, slower-moving channel: it tightens the legal buyer pool for Iranian barrels, which traders read as an escalation signal on top of the physical disruption. That combination of a physical scare and a policy escalation is why both benchmarks jumped again in after-hours trading rather than fading once the initial headline was digested."
