  "deepDive": {
    "title": "Oil Shocks and the Yield Curve: Why a Tanker Attack Can Flatten What a Jobs Report Just Steepened",
    "content": [
      "Monday's soft June payrolls report (just 57,000 jobs) pulled the 2-year Treasury yield down and steepened the curve, the market's way of pricing a Fed that might cut sooner. One day later, an Iranian strike on tankers near the Strait of Hormuz did the opposite: the 2-year rose 4 bps to 4.15% while the 10-year barely moved, narrowing the 2s10s spread back down to roughly 0.35 points. Two macro data points, two opposite curve reactions, 24 hours apart — a clean illustration of why the curve's shape on any given day tells you more about which shock is currently dominant than about a settled consensus on where rates are headed.",
      "The mechanism runs through inflation expectations at different horizons. The Strait of Hormuz carries about a fifth of the world's oil, so an attack there — even one that damages no refineries and destroys no barrels outright — immediately reprices near-term inflation risk, because energy costs feed quickly into headline CPI and consumer expectations. The 2-year yield is unusually sensitive to that kind of near-horizon repricing because it's essentially a bet on the average fed funds rate over the next two years; a supply shock that could keep the Fed from cutting shows up there first. The 10-year, by contrast, reflects a longer-run blend of growth and inflation expectations that a single tanker incident rarely moves much, since markets generally expect oil shocks of this kind to fade rather than compound.",
      "Historically, central banks have tried to 'look through' oil-driven inflation spikes precisely because they're supply shocks rather than demand overheating — the 1990 Gulf War oil spike and the 2011 Libya disruption both saw the Fed largely hold its framework rather than react to the price level alone. What's different and harder to look through this time is that the Hormuz disruption isn't a one-off: it's the latest flare-up in a shooting war that's been intermittently closing the strait since February, layered directly on top of a labor market that just gave the Fed its softest print in four months. That layering is what makes Wednesday's FOMC minutes more consequential than a routine release — the committee has to signal whether it still treats geopolitical oil spikes as transitory when the underlying conflict keeps reopening.",
      "For anyone reading tomorrow's minutes, the thing to watch isn't the headline decision — no one expects a meeting-day move from minutes alone — but the language around the 2s10s dynamic itself. If the Fed's internal discussion leans toward treating the oil spike as noise, expect the curve to re-steepen quickly as the 2-year gives back today's gain; if the minutes show governors flagging persistent geopolitical risk to inflation, the front end could stay elevated even as growth data continues to soften. That's the practical read-through of today's brief: the S&P's chip-driven 0.45% dip and the oil-driven curve flattening are two symptoms of the same underlying tension — markets trying to price growth risk (soft jobs, AI valuation doubts) and supply risk (Hormuz) at the same time, with no clean way to net them into a single signal yet."
    ]
  }
